            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Karen Grant"
            },
            "reviewBody": "These were delicious. I made them ahead of time by boiling them, draining the water and adding the butter and brown sugar. I didn't add any pepper. Before serving, I put them in the oven to warm them up and thicken the sauce. Delicious."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "LITTLEFATCAT"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I steamed the carrots instead of boiling them. Instead of turning the heat to low after adding the sauce ingredients, I recommend turning the heat up a bit and cooking until the sauce reduces and carmelizes...just be sure to stir it frequently to keep it from burning. This way all the good sauce stays on the carrots instead of in the pan. I also added a pinch of crushed red pepper...I like a sweet/hot combination. Really very good!",
            "datePublished": "2001-06-11T12:35:59.187Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "TRACYDBURT"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Great recipe! I left out the pepper and added a little cinnamon instead. It was a huge hit and is sure to become a staple at the dinner table.",
            "datePublished": "2003-02-24T14:21:58.467Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "LiivHappy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Tasty and sweet and easy to make! For a potluck gathering I cut up approximately 4 lbs of carrots and made 4 times the sauce. Cooked the carrots just to the point I could pierce with a fork, drained and set aside in my dutch oven. Cooked the sauce separately, poured over carrots, covered and let them sit for a couple hours while I worked on other dishes. Hour before dinner put them in the oven at low heat and then broiled for 5 - 10 minutes stirring often. I drained the sauce and served, they were sweet and perfect and not mushy at all! (Sauce is great to keep in the pot too if you like!!)",
            "datePublished": "2016-10-10T13:52:40.340Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Diana Lee Sherman"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Very good! I also turned up the heat to caramelize the brown sugar.... delicious!",
            "datePublished": "2025-02-18T02:12:14.561Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Allrecipes Member"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I prefer to melt the butter and brown sugar separately with the salt and pepper, then toss with the cooked carrots.",
            "datePublished": "2024-01-07T23:46:18.026Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Susan Nelson"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This has been a staple in our family for years--but with variations. I don't boil them, but instead sautee'/steam them in a covered skillet, using a small amount of water and butter. Then I sometimes add things like pecans and dried cranberries and/or toasted coconut. Stir them in while cooking, the pecans become toasted, too!",
            "datePublished": "2017-04-12T15:29:15.683Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "fruitdog"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I cut mine in 2\" pieces and then in half lengthwise and cooked less. Twenty minutes for whole carrots would still be too long for our tastes. I used less brown sugar for taste, the hubbs loved them whereas he normally isn't a fan. He even had leftovers. Will make these again!",
            "datePublished": "2022-10-01T22:04:20.765Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
